.PP
A comma or a tab alone is not a table: a log line with a comma in it stays a line. An unnamed CSV the first lines do not show as one opens with \fB\-\-format csv\fR; the Info panel's notes say so. With \fB\-\-format csv\fR, \fBtsv\fR or \fBpsv\fR and no \fB\-\-compression\fR, compression still comes from the first bytes.
.SH "FORMAT SPECS"
A format spec is a TOML file that describes a binary format, or a family of delimited text files, so datui opens it as a table.
.PP
\fB\fBl2feed.toml\fR\fR
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
name = "acme.l2feed"
description = "Level 2 capture"
match = { glob = ["*.l2"], magic = "L2FD" }
endian = "le"

[header]
fields = [{ name = "magic", type = "str", size = 4 }, { name = "count", type = "u1" }]

[records]
count = "header.count"
fields = [
  { name = "ts",     type = "u8", time = "ns" },
  { name = "symbol", type = "str", size = 8 },
  { name = "side",   type = "u1", enum = { 1 = "BUY", 2 = "SELL" } },
  { name = "price",  type = "u4", scale = 4, null = "max" },
]
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fB\fBmake_day_l2.py\fR\fR writes \fBday.l2\fR, a file in that format:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import ctypes


class Header(ctypes.LittleEndianStructure):
    _layout_ = "ms"
    _pack_ = 1  # no padding between fields
    _fields_ = [
        ("magic", ctypes.c_char * 4),
        ("count", ctypes.c_uint8),
    ]


class Record(ctypes.LittleEndianStructure):
    _layout_ = "ms"
    _pack_ = 1
    _fields_ = [
        ("ts", ctypes.c_uint64),  # nanoseconds since 1970
        ("symbol", ctypes.c_char * 8),
        ("side", ctypes.c_uint8),  # 1 BUY, 2 SELL
        ("price", ctypes.c_uint32),  # in ten\-thousandths; the largest value is null
    ]


records = [
    Record(1709294400000000000, b"MSFT", 1, 4105000),
    Record(1709294400000500000, b"AAPL", 2, 0xFFFFFFFF),
]
with open("day.l2", "wb") as f:
    f.write(bytes(Header(b"L2FD", len(records))))
    for record in records:
        f.write(bytes(record))
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
python3 make_day_l2.py
datui formats check ./l2feed.toml day.l2
datui \-\-format ./l2feed.toml day.l2
mkdir \-p formats
cp l2feed.toml formats/
DATUI_FORMATS_PATH=formats datui day.l2
DATUI_FORMATS_PATH=formats datui formats
.EE
.RE
.TP
\fBdatui formats check ./l2feed.toml day.l2\fR
Checks the spec and prints the file's header and first rows
.TP
\fBdatui \-\-format ./l2feed.toml day.l2\fR
Reads the file with that spec file
.TP
\fBdatui \-\-format acme.l2feed day.l2\fR
Reads it with the spec of that name, from the search path
.TP
\fBdatui day.l2\fR
Reads it with the spec whose \fBmatch\fR it fits, from the search path
.TP
\fBdatui formats\fR
Lists the specs and dictionaries on the search path
.PP
A spec reads fixed-size records, records that carry their length, several message types in one stream, or compressed blocks; a \fBkind = "delimited"\fR spec reads CSV-like text with lines above its header. Specs are data: no scripts or expressions, and every size read from a file is bounded. \fB\-\-format\fR also takes a spec's \fBhttp(s)://\fR, \fBs3://\fR, \fBgs://\fR or \fBaz://\fR URL, fetched once as the open starts; a spec file is at most 1 MiB.
.SS "A spec"
\fBl2feed.toml\fR above is a whole spec. Its table has the columns \fBts\fR (a datetime), \fBsymbol\fR, \fBside\fR and \fBprice\fR (a decimal with four places, null where the field holds its largest value), one row per record. Format spec reference lists every field type and key.
.TP
\fBname\fR
The format's name, namespaced: \fBacme.l2feed\fR. \fB\-\-format\fR takes it
.TP
\fBdescription\fR
Shown by \fBdatui formats\fR
.TP
\fBmatch\fR
Which files are this format: \fBglob\fR (a pattern or a list), \fBmagic\fR (a string or a list of bytes) at \fBmagic_offset\fR (default 0), \fBwhere\fR (header values)
.TP
\fBendian\fR
\fBle\fR (default), \fBbe\fR, or \fBauto\fR: big-endian when the magic (at least two bytes) reads reversed. For fields without their own suffix
.TP
\fBlayout\fR
\fBrows\fR (default): one file of records. \fBcolumns\fR: a directory with one file per field
.TP
\fB[header] fields\fR
Fields read once from the start of the file. Later parts refer to them
.TP
\fB[header] size\fR
The header's size when it is more than its fields; a number or a header field
.TP
\fB[records] fields\fR
The fields of one record, in order
.TP
\fB[records] size\fR
The record's size, at least the fields' sum (the default); the rest is skipped
.TP
\fB[records] count\fR
How many records there are, such as \fB"header.count"\fR or \fB"footer.n"\fR
.TP
\fB[records] framing\fR
\fBfixed\fR (default), \fBlength_prefixed\fR, \fBvariant\fR or \fBsync\fR: see records of different sizes
.TP
\fB[records] ring\fR
A ring buffer of fixed records: the oldest record's index, such as \fB"header.write_idx"\fR; rows start there and wrap
.TP
\fB[records] checksum\fR
A checksum in each record: see checks
.TP
\fB[[variants]]\fR
Record layouts a type field picks: see variants
.TP
\fB[footer]\fR
Fields at the end of the file: see footer
.TP
\fB[blocks]\fR
Records in blocks, each compressed on its own: see blocks
.TP
\fB[sections.NAME]\fR
A part of the file, \fBoffset\fR and \fBsize\fR from the header, that \fBstring_at\fR fields point into
.TP
\fB[capture]\fR
Records in the UDP payloads of a pcap or pcapng capture: see captures
.TP
\fB[files]\fR
A directory tree of the format's files as one table: see a tree of files
.SS "Where specs live"
Datui reads every \fB*.toml\fR in these places, in order. The first spec of each name wins, as with \fBPATH\fR.
.TP
\fB~/.config/datui/formats/\fR
Your own specs
.TP
\fB$DATUI_FORMATS_PATH\fR
Directories separated by \fB:\fR (\fB;\fR on Windows), such as a checked-out repository of a team's specs
.TP
\fB[formats] path\fR in config
More directories. Lists add up across imported config files
.PP
\fBdatui formats\fR lists each spec, what it matches, the file it came from, any copy of the same name it overrides, and the files that could not be read, with the line and column of each problem. The same places hold FIX log dictionaries: QuickFIX XML files and TOML files of \fBkind = "fix"\fR, listed after the specs.
.PP
\fBdatui formats check SPEC [FILE]\fR checks one spec, by name or file. With a file, it prints the warnings and the first ten rows. Given a QuickFIX dictionary, it checks that, and with a FIX log says how many messages it matches and which of its tags they hold. It exits non-zero on an error, so a repository of specs can run it in CI.
.SS "Which spec reads a file"
.TP
\fB\-\-format FILE\fR: a path (it has a \fB/\fR or ends \fB.toml\fR), or an \fBhttp(s)://\fR, \fBs3://\fR, \fBgs://\fR or \fBaz://\fR URL fetched once as the open starts
That spec, whatever the file is called
.TP
\fB\-\-format NAME\fR
The spec of that name
.TP
A name datui already reads (\fB.csv\fR, \fB.parquet\fR)
Read as it is, as before, unless a delimited spec matches a \fB.csv\fR, \fB.tsv\fR or \fB.psv\fR
.TP
A \fBglob\fR matches
That spec
.TP
A \fBmagic\fR matches, in a file whose bytes are no format datui reads (such as Parquet)
That spec
.PP
A spec with \fBmatch.where\fR matches only a file whose header holds those values, so one spec per version can share a glob and a magic. In a spec, in place of its \fBmatch\fR line:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
match = { glob = "*.l2", magic = "L2FD", where = { "header.version" = 3 } }
.EE
.RE
.PP
When two specs match the same way, the first on the search path reads the file. The bar shows \fB2 formats match\fR, and the Notes tab names the others. A file no spec matches opens as it does without specs; a local file no reader takes either opens in the hex view, where \fBB\fR reads it with a spec and \fBr\fR lines the bytes up in records while you write one.
.PP
\fBb\fR on the table picks another spec and reads the file again with it. The Notes tab of \fBi\fR says which spec read the file and why, the header's values, and any bytes left out.
.SS "Delimited text"
Loggers and instruments write a metadata line and a units line above a padded header. A spec of \fBkind = "delimited"\fR holds the CSV options for such a family of files, so they open with no flags: from the command line, from the home screen, compressed, or as a directory.
.PP
\fB\fBinstrument.toml\fR\fR
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
name = "acme.instrument\-log"
kind = "delimited"
match = { magic = "#device_info" }
comment = "#"
skip_initial_space = true
header_rows = { name = 3, unit = 2 }
metadata_line = 1

[columns]
time = { from = ["Lcl Date", "Lcl Time", "UTCOfst"], as = "datetime" }
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fB\fBflight.csv\fR\fR
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#device_info, log_version="1.03", model="Unit 7, rev B", serial="123"
#yyyy\-mm\-dd, hh:mm:ss, hh:mm, degrees, volts, deg F
  Lcl Date, Lcl Time, UTCOfst,     Latitude, bus1volts, T1 Temp
          ,         ,        ,             ,      25.1,   187.2
2024\-03\-01, 10:00:00,  \-05:00,    40.100000,      25.0,   180.0
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
datui formats check ./instrument.toml flight.csv
datui \-\-format ./instrument.toml flight.csv
.EE
.RE
.PP
A delimited spec takes the keys of the config's [\fB[csv]\fR](.\:.\:/\:reference/\:settings.\:md#csv), plus \fBmatch\fR, \fBkind\fR, the layout keys and \fB[columns]\fR.
.TP
\fBkind\fR
\fBdelimited\fR. Default \fBbinary\fR
.TP
\fBmatch\fR
\fBglob\fR and \fBmagic\fR, as for other format specs. \fBmagic\fR compares the start of the first line
.TP
\fBdelimiter\fR
One character, \fB"tab"\fR, \fB"\et"\fR or a code such as \fB"0x1f"\fR, as \fB\-\-delimiter\fR takes. Default \fB,\fR, or the one the file's name implies
.TP
\fBcomment\fR
Lines that start with it are skipped wherever they are
.TP
\fBskip_initial_space\fR
\fBtrue\fR: ignore the spaces after a delimiter
.TP
\fBheader_rows\fR
\fB{ name = N, unit = M }\fR: the line that names the columns and the line that gives their units. \fBname\fR may be a list of lines, joined with \fBheader_join\fR (default a space). A number or a list is \fBname\fR alone. A header line is never data
.TP
\fBheader_join\fR
What joins the pieces of a name from several lines
.TP
\fBmetadata_line\fR
A line of \fBkey="value"\fR or \fBkey=value\fR pairs, separated by commas, for the Info panel. It must not be data: above the last header line, within \fBskip_lines\fR, or a comment line
.TP
\fBnull_values\fR
A value, or a list, read as null: \fB"NA"\fR, or \fB"COL=\-999"\fR for one column
.TP
\fBskip_lines\fR
Lines to pass over before the header
.TP
\fB[columns]\fR
Derived columns, below
.PP
Lines count from 1 at the top of the file. Each option the spec sets replaces the config's; a flag typed on the command line (\fB\-\-delimiter\fR, \fB\-\-comment\fR, \fB\-\-skip\-initial\-space\fR, \fB\-\-header\-rows\fR, \fB\-\-skip\-lines\fR) wins over the spec. The options the spec does not set keep theirs. \fBdatui \-\-delimiter ';' formats check SPEC FILE\fR reads the file as an open with those flags would, and names the flags that override the spec. The header lines and the metadata line are the only lines read apart from the CSV reader.
.PP
\fBUnits\fR
.PP
A unit sits beside its column's type on the table's type row (\fBf64 \(pc deg F\fR), in a \fBUnit\fR column on the Info panel's Schema tab, and in chart axis titles (\fBT1 Temp (deg F)\fR). A filter, sort or drill keeps them, and so does a query, pivot or melt for each column it carries unchanged, renamed or not. A column a query computes has no unit, even under the name of one that had.
.PP
\fBMetadata\fR
.PP
The Info panel's \fBMetadata\fR tab lists the metadata line's pairs, under its leading item when it has one (\fBdevice_info\fR). A line that is not pairs is shown as it is. For a directory, the first file's line is shown.
.PP
\fBDerived columns\fR
.TP
\fBdatetime\fR
from: a date and a time, and optionally a UTC offset such as \fB\-05:00\fR, \fB+0530\fR or \fB\-5\fR; or one column of text
.br
Column: A datetime. With an offset it is in UTC
.TP
\fBdate\fR
from: one column
.br
Column: A date
.TP
\fBtime\fR
from: one column
.br
Column: A time of day
.PP
\fBformat = "%Y\-%m\-%d %H:%M:%S"\fR gives the strftime format of the text, a date and a time joined with a space; without it the format is inferred. A value that does not parse is null. The column goes before the first column it is made from, which stays; one named after a column it is made from replaces that column, and its unit. There is no expression language: anything more is a query.
.PP
\fBMatching\fR
.PP
A delimited spec matches a file whose name says no format datui reads, or says \fB.csv\fR, \fB.tsv\fR or \fB.psv\fR, compressed or not. A directory is read through the spec its first file matches. \fBH\fR on the Info panel's Schema tab reads the file without a header, and without its derived columns.
.SS "Checks"
.TP
A field, size or key the spec gets wrong
The spec is refused, with its line and column
.TP
The magic does not match
The open fails, showing the bytes found
.TP
A file that ends partway through a record
The whole records open; a note shows the bytes left over
.TP
A header count larger than the file
The whole records open, with a note
.TP
A record whose length or type cannot be read
The records before it open; a note says where the rest was left out
.TP
\fBchecksum\fR in \fB[records]\fR
A \fBchecksum_ok\fR column, true or false for each record, rather than an error
.TP
A spec for a file in a bucket or at a URL
The file is downloaded first, then read
.PP
In a spec's \fB[records]\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
checksum = { algo = "crc16\-ccitt", field = "crc", from = "len", to = "crc" }
.EE
.RE
.PP
A record checksum covers the bytes from the \fBfrom\fR field (default: the record's start) up to the \fBto\fR field (default: the checksum's own field). \fBalgo\fR is \fBcrc16\-ccitt\fR, \fBcrc16\-xmodem\fR, \fBcrc16\-modbus\fR, \fBcrc16\-arc\fR, \fBcrc32\fR, \fBcrc32c\fR, \fBsum8\fR or \fBxor8\fR; a footer checksum takes the same names.
.SS "Compressed files"
\fBday.l2.zst\fR, \fB.gz\fR, \fB.bz2\fR and \fB.xz\fR are decompressed to a temporary file before they are read: the loading screen says \fBDecompressing\fR, then \fBReading records\fR, and Esc stops either. The glob matches the name without the compression suffix, and magic is read from the decompressed bytes.
.SS "Large files"
A spec's file is read lazy, or converted once when compressed. It is memory-mapped, and only the columns and rows on screen are decoded. Records that are not all one size, and blocks, are indexed by one pass when the file opens. That pass keeps where each record starts (5 bytes a record, up to 64M records), so a query reads every column from there rather than walking the records again, and a file opened again with the same spec is not walked again. Scrolling to the last row of a gigabyte file reads only the rows shown. A sort, filter, query, chart or analysis reads every row of the columns it uses, a batch at a time on the streaming engine (\fB[performance] streaming\fR, on by default). A table holds at most 4,294,967,295 rows; records past that are not shown, and the dataset's notes say so.
.PP
A file that grows while it is open keeps the rows it had; open it again to read the rest. A file cut short by another program is refused at the next read rather than read past its end.
.SH "FORMAT SPEC REFERENCE"
Every field type and key a format spec takes. Each example below is a whole spec; \fBdatui formats check ./spec.toml\fR checks one.
.SS "Field types"
Type names follow Kaitai Struct <\fIhttps://kaitai.io\fR>. Widths are in bytes.
.TP
\fBu1\fR to \fBu8\fR, \fBs1\fR to \fBs8\fR
Unsigned and signed integers of 1 to 8 bytes, \fBu3\fR and \fBs6\fR included
.TP
\fBf2\fR, \fBf4\fR, \fBf8\fR
Floats; \fBf2\fR is a half float
.TP
\fBbf2\fR
A bfloat16
.TP
\fBvu\fR, \fBvs\fR
LEB128 varints, \fBvs\fR zigzag-encoded. Records only
.TP
\fBbool\fR
One byte, nonzero is true
.TP
\fBstr\fR
Text of \fBsize\fR bytes, its NUL and space padding trimmed
.TP
\fBstrz\fR
Text up to a NUL, at most \fBsize\fR bytes when given. Records only
.TP
\fBbytes\fR
Raw bytes of \fBsize\fR
.TP
\fBpad\fR
\fBsize\fR bytes skipped, no column
.PP
A \fBle\fR or \fBbe\fR suffix (\fBu4be\fR, \fBs2le\fR, \fBbf2be\fR) overrides the spec's \fBendian\fR.
.SS "Field keys"
.TP
\fBname\fR
The column's name. Every field but \fBpad\fR has one
.br
Example: \fB"price"\fR
.TP
\fBsize\fR
Bytes of a \fBstr\fR, \fBstrz\fR, \fBbytes\fR or \fBpad\fR: a number, a header or footer field, an earlier field of the record, or \fBrest\fR, what is left of the record
.br
Example: \fB8\fR, \fB"header.len"\fR, \fB"len"\fR, \fB"rest"\fR
.TP
\fBsize_adjust\fR
Added to a size read from a field
.br
Example: \fB\-4\fR
.TP
\fBencoding\fR
Of a \fBstr\fR or \fBstrz\fR: \fButf8\fR (default), \fBlatin1\fR, \fButf16le\fR or \fButf16be\fR
.br
Example: \fB"latin1"\fR
.TP
\fBcount\fR
That many values side by side: one Array column
.br
Example: \fB10\fR
.TP
\fBflatten\fR
With \fBcount\fR (up to 1024), columns \fBname_0\fR to \fBname_9\fR instead of an Array
.br
Example: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
\fBnull\fR
A stored value that means no value: the type's smallest or largest value, a NaN, or this number, which the type must be able to hold
.br
Example: \fB"min"\fR, \fB"max"\fR, \fB"nan"\fR, \fB\-1\fR
.TP
\fBscale\fR
Implied decimal places: the integer becomes a Decimal
.br
Example: \fB4\fR
.TP
\fBfactor\fR, \fBoffset\fR
\fBvalue * factor + offset\fR, as a float
.br
Example: \fB0.1\fR, \fB\-40.0\fR
.TP
\fBenum\fR
Codes and labels; an unlisted code reads as its number
.br
Example: \fB{ 1 = "BUY", 2 = "SELL" }\fR
.TP
\fBtime\fR
A count of \fBdays\fR, \fBs\fR, \fBms\fR, \fBus\fR or \fBns\fR: a datetime, or a date for \fBdays\fR. A float counts fractions too
.br
Example: \fB"ns"\fR
.TP
\fBepoch\fR
What the count is since. Default 1970-01-01
.br
Example: \fB2000\-01\-01\fR
.TP
\fBdate\fR
An integer such as 20240102, as a date
.br
Example: \fB"yyyymmdd"\fR
.TP
\fBof_day\fR
With \fBtime\fR, a count since midnight: a time of day
.br
Example: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
\fBdate\fR with \fBof_day\fR
The day those times are on, from a header field that reads as a date (\fBdate = "yyyymmdd"\fR, \fBtime = "days"\fR), a datetime, or text such as \fB2024\-01\-02\fR: a datetime
.br
Example: \fB"header.trade_date"\fR
.TP
\fBfile\fR
In the columns layout, the file in the directory holding the field. Default: its name
.br
Example: \fB"px.dat"\fR
.TP
\fBoffset\fR
In the columns layout, where the column starts in one file: see columns layout
.br
Example: \fB"header.px_off"\fR
.TP
\fBlookup\fR
An integer indexes a list of symbols in a file beside the data: a categorical. \fBformat\fR is \fBlines\fR (default), \fBnul\fR or \fBstr:N\fR
.br
Example: \fB{ file = "../sym", format = "lines" }\fR
.PP
These keys are for record fields only:
.TP
\fBdelta\fR
Each value is the change from the record before; the running sum is shown. \fB"block"\fR starts the sum again in each block
.br
Example: \fBtrue\fR, \fB"block"\fR
.TP
\fBbits\fR
Bit fields of an integer, each its own column. A width of 1 is a bool
.br
Example: \fB[{ name = "valid", bit = 0 }, { name = "mode", bit = 4, width = 3, enum = { 0 = "IDLE" } }]\fR
.TP
\fBgroup\fR
A counted run of items: a List of Structs. Takes no \fBtype\fR
.br
Example: \fB{ count = "n_levels", fields = [...] }\fR
.TP
\fBstring_at\fR
An unsigned offset into a \fB[sections.strings]\fR part of the file, where NUL-terminated text is
.br
Example: \fB"strings"\fR
.PP
A field takes at most one of \fBtime\fR (or \fBdate\fR), \fBscale\fR, \fBfactor\fR and \fBenum\fR.
.PP
A field refers to an earlier one by name, never by an expression. In the header, \fBsize = "len"\fR reads an earlier header field; anywhere, \fBheader.NAME\fR and \fBfooter.NAME\fR do. In a record, \fB"len"\fR reads an earlier field of the same record. A record whose own field gives its size is \fBlength_prefixed\fR.
.SS "Records of different sizes"
.RS 4
.EX
name = "acme.messages"
match = { glob = "*.msg" }

[records]
framing = "length_prefixed"
size = "len"          # the field that holds each record's length
size_adjust = 2       # the length leaves out its own two bytes
fields = [{ name = "len", type = "u2" }, { name = "msg", type = "str", size = "rest" }]
.EE
.RE
.TP
\fBfixed\fR
Every record takes \fBsize\fR, or what its fields take
.TP
\fBlength_prefixed\fR
A field of the record gives its size: \fBsize = "len"\fR, with \fBsize_adjust\fR
.TP
\fBvariant\fR
The variant the type field picks gives the size: its \fBsize\fR, or what its fields take
.TP
\fBsync\fR
Each record starts with the \fBsync\fR marker (\fB"1ACFFC1D"\fR, \fB"0xEB90"\fR or a list of bytes); bytes between records are skipped and counted in a note
.TP
\fBlength_suffix\fR
\fBtrue\fR: the length is written again after the record, as Fortran unformatted files do. Needs \fBsize = "len"\fR
.TP
\fBalign\fR
Each record starts at a multiple of this (1 to 65536), counted from the first: \fBalign = 2\fR for IFF and RIFF chunks
.PP
Records that are not all one size are walked once when the file opens, and the start of every 1024th is kept, so a scroll anywhere reads from the nearest one.
.PP
\fBVariants\fR
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
name = "acme.orders"
match = { glob = "*.ord" }

[records]
framing = "length_prefixed"
size = "len"
size_adjust = 2
fields = [{ name = "len", type = "u2" }, { name = "kind", type = "str", size = 1 }]
type = "kind"

[[variants]]
name = "add"
when = "A"
fields = [{ name = "ref", type = "u8" }, { name = "shares", type = "u4" }, { name = "price", type = "u4", scale = 4 }]

[[variants]]
name = "exec"
when = ["E", "C"]
fields = [{ name = "ref", type = "u8" }, { name = "shares", type = "u4" }]
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBfields\fR (or \fB[records.common] fields\fR) are the fields every record starts with. \fBtype\fR names the common field that picks the variant: an integer, or text, compared with its padding trimmed. \fBtype = { field = "kind", type = "u1" }\fR declares it in place.
.TP
\fBname\fR
Shown in the \fBtype\fR column
.TP
\fBwhen\fR
The type value, or a list of them, that picks it
.TP
\fBfields\fR
The fields after the common ones
.TP
\fBsize\fR, \fBsize_adjust\fR
The whole record's size, when more than its fields take
.PP
All records make one table, with a \fBtype\fR column naming each one's variant. A column of a field one variant lacks is null in that variant's rows; a field two variants share is one column, so it must be the same field in both. A record of a type no variant names shows as \fB?X\fR when its size is known (\fBlength_prefixed\fR); otherwise the read stops there, with a note.
.PP
\fBdatui \-\-table add day.ord\fR opens one variant as its own table: only its records and its columns.
.PP
On the home screen, a file a spec's glob names that holds several variants counts them as tables ("2 tables" in its details). Enter opens every record; \(-> lists the tables inside it, one row each at \fBday.ord/add\fR, and Enter on one opens it alone. That path opens the table on the command line too, and is what recents record.
.SS "Footer"
.RS 4
.EX
name = "acme.counted"
match = { glob = "*.cnt" }

[records]
count = "footer.n"
fields = [{ name = "v", type = "u2" }]

[footer]
fields = [{ name = "n", type = "u4" }, { name = "crc", type = "u4" }]
checksum = { algo = "crc32", field = "crc" }
.EE
.RE
.PP
The footer is read from the end of the file, so its fields' sizes are written in the spec, or it gives \fBsize\fR. Later parts refer to its fields as \fBfooter.NAME\fR: a record count, or a block index's offset. \fBchecksum\fR checks the bytes before the footer against a footer field; a mismatch is a note.
.SS "Blocks"
.RS 4
.EX
name = "acme.blocks"
match = { glob = "*.blk" }

[blocks]
header = [{ name = "clen", type = "u4" }, { name = "rawlen", type = "u4" }]
size = "clen"
compression = "zstd"
uncompressed = "rawlen"

[records]
fields = [{ name = "v", type = "u4", delta = "block" }]
.EE
.RE
.PP
The data after the file's header is a run of blocks: a block header, then \fBsize\fR bytes of records. The records' framing applies inside each block.
.TP
\fBheader\fR
The fields at the start of each block
.TP
\fBsize\fR, \fBsize_adjust\fR
The bytes after the block header: a number or a block header field
.TP
\fBcompression\fR
\fBnone\fR (default), \fBgzip\fR, \fBdeflate\fR, \fBzlib\fR, \fBzstd\fR, \fBlz4\fR, \fBlz4_block\fR, \fBsnappy\fR, \fBsnappy_framed\fR, \fBbrotli\fR, \fBbzip2\fR or \fBxz\fR. Or a code in the block header: \fB{ field = "codec", values = { 0 = "none", 1 = "zstd" } }\fR
.TP
\fBuncompressed\fR
The block header field with the decompressed size. Required for \fBlz4_block\fR
.TP
\fBrecords\fR
The block header field counting its records; missing records are null
.TP
\fBindex\fR
\fB{ at = "footer.index_off", count = "footer.n_blocks", fields = [...] }\fR: a block index read instead of walking the blocks. Its entries need an integer \fBoffset\fR, and may give \fBrows\fR
.PP
Only the block headers are read when the file opens. A block is decompressed when its rows are first read, up to 256 MiB each, and the last few are kept. A block that will not decompress is left out, with a note.
.SS "Captures"
.RS 4
.EX
name = "acme.multicast"
match = { glob = "*.pcap" }

[capture]
header = [{ name = "session", type = "str", size = 10 }, { name = "seq", type = "u8" }, { name = "count", type = "u2" }]
count = "count"
time = "captured"

[records]
fields = [{ name = "price", type = "u4" }]
.EE
.RE
.PP
The file is a pcap or pcapng capture, told apart by its magic. Each UDP payload holds the records, after the payload \fBheader\fR; \fBcount\fR names the header field counting them, and \fBtime\fR adds a column with each packet's capture time. Packets that are not UDP are left out and counted in a note. A capture spec has no \fB[header]\fR, \fB[footer]\fR or \fB[blocks]\fR.
.SS "A tree of files"
.RS 4
.EX
name = "acme.trades"
match = { glob = "*.bin" }

[files]
path = "{date:%Y%m%d}/{venue}/trades.bin"

[records]
fields = [{ name = "price", type = "f8" }]
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBdatui \-\-format acme.trades store/\fR reads every file under \fBstore/\fR that the pattern matches as one table, with a column for each part: a date for a part with a format, text otherwise. A file whose part does not parse as its date is left out, with a note.
.SS "Columns layout"
.RS 4
.EX
name = "kdb.trades"
layout = "columns"
endian = "be"

[records]
fields = [{ name = "price", type = "f8" }, { name = "size", type = "s8" }]
.EE
.RE
.PP
With it, \fBdatui \-\-format kdb.trades db/trades/\fR reads \fBdb/trades/price\fR and \fBdb/trades/size\fR as two columns of one table, as kdb+ splays a table. A \fB[header]\fR describes the start of each file. A glob in a columns spec matches the directory.
.PP
When the header lists where each column starts in one file, every field gives \fBoffset\fR and the spec reads that one file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
name = "acme.packed"
layout = "columns"

[header]
fields = [{ name = "n", type = "u4" }, { name = "px_off", type = "u4" }]

[records]
count = "header.n"
fields = [{ name = "px", type = "f8", offset = "header.px_off" }]
.EE
.RE
